<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alfred the Great</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/849">849</a>–899</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Alfred of Wessex</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alfred the Great was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> from 871 to 899. After defeating the Danish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-heathen-army">Great Heathen Army</a> at Edington in 878, he built fortified burhs, reorganised the army and a fleet, and promoted learning and translation into English. His biographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asser">Asser</a> records that several Welsh kings, including those of Dyfed, Brycheiniog and Gwent, submitted to him for protection against their neighbours.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great.jpg" alt="Alfred the Great" width="320" height="312" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG - Classical Numismatic Group · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alfred_the_Great,_silver_penny;_struck_875%E2%80%93880_(obverse).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Alfred was the youngest son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelwulf-of-wessex">Æthelwulf of Wessex</a> and his first wife Osburh. His grandfather Ecgberht became king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> in 802. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asser">Asser</a>, writing in 893, says Alfred was born at Wantage in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berkshire">Berkshire</a>, though the West Saxon genealogical lists imply a slightly earlier date, and historians differ over the exact year. His public life began in 865, at 16, when Æthelred came to the throne, and Asser gave him the title secundarius, perhaps marking him as a recognised successor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Æthelred died in April 871, leaving two under-age sons, and Alfred became king in accordance with an agreement the brothers had made earlier that year at Swinbeorg. After his victory at Edington in 878 he made an agreement with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> dividing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> territory and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danelaw">Danelaw</a>, and oversaw the conversion of the Viking leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guthrum">Guthrum</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. In 886 he began styling himself King of the Anglo-Saxons after reoccupying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>.</p><p>In the late 880s or early 890s he issued a law code, a domboc, combining his own laws with those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ine-of-wessex">Ine of Wessex</a> in 120 chapters. He said he kept the laws that pleased him and rejected others on the advice of his councillors. He also began a revival of learning, perhaps inspired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, recruiting scholars from Mercia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> and abroad and having <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> works the king thought most necessary for all men to know translated into English. A chronicle of the rise of his house gave the West Saxon kings a genealogy back to Adam.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Alfred married Ealhswith, daughter of a Mercian nobleman, in 868. Their children included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-elder">Edward the Elder</a>, who succeeded him, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelflaed">Æthelflæd</a>, who became lady of the Mercians. He died on 26 October 899 at 50 or 51; how he died is unknown, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asser">Asser</a>'s description of a lifelong painful illness has led modern doctors to suggest Crohn's disease or haemorrhoids. His will is the oldest to survive of any English king. He has been called the Great since at least the 13th century, though the epithet was popularised only from the 16th, and he is the only native-born English monarch given it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alfred the Great” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son Baldwin II (879–918), who married Ælfthryth, daughter of <b>Alfred the Great</b>, used the disorder of the Viking age to build fortresses and gather counties, abbeys and royal lands into a single principality.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-flanders">County of Flanders · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Alfred the Great</b> (r. 871–899) held off the Great Heathen Army and fortified Wessex with a network of burhs, and his son Edward the Elder and daughter Æthelflæd, Lady of the Mercians, reconquered the Danelaw south of the Humber in the 910s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…quality, including the carvings at Breedon on the Hill and the church at Brixworth, and Mercian scholars mattered to the later English revival: <b>Alfred the Great</b> recruited four Mercian clerics, among them Wærferth of Worcester and Plegmund, later archbishop of Canterbury, to help with his programme of learning.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mercia">Kingdom of Mercia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Alfred the Great</b> (r. 871–899) strengthened royal government: his law book drew on the codes of Ine, Æthelberht of Kent and Offa of Mercia, his burhs created a lasting framework of defence and administration, and he presented himself as king of the Anglo-Saxons, all the English not under…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wessex">Kingdom of Wessex · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 9th century the Vikings overran Northumbria south of the Tyne and most of Mercia, and <b>Alfred the Great</b> joined the part of Mercia that held out and all of Kent to Wessex.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-english">Old English · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>In England the Great Heathen Army landed in 865, took York, killed King Edmund of East Anglia in 869 and overran Northumbria, East Anglia and much of Mercia, until <b>Alfred the Great</b> of the Kingdom of Wessex defeated Guthrum at Edington in 878.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Wantage, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winchester">Winchester</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.59,-1.43&amp;year=881" aria-label="Wantage on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Alfred the Great"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4924 5716h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4924 5716h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.59,-1.43&amp;year=881">What was here in 881 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/aethelwulf-of-wessex"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/aethelwulf-of-wessex.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Æthelwulf of Wessex</span><span class="tp">Father &#183; d. 858</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/aethelflaed"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/aethelflaed.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Æthelflæd</span><span class="tp">Child &#183; c. 870–918</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-elder"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/edward-the-elder.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Edward the Elder</span><span class="tp">Child &#183; c. 874–924</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/aelfthryth-countess-of-flanders"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/aelfthryth-countess-of-flanders.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Ælfthryth, Countess of Flanders</span><span class="tp">Child &#183; c. 877–929</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…a king's raw material and instruments of rule are a well-peopled land, and he must have men of prayer, men of war, and men of work.&#8221;</p><footer>King Alfred's Boethius, chapter 17 &#183; tr. W. J. Sedgefield, 1900 &#183; Alfred's own addition to Boethius &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/King_Alfred's_Version_of_the_Consolations_of_Boethius/The_Consolations_of_Boethius" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…it has ever been my desire to live honourably while I was alive, and after my death to leave to them that should come after me my memory in good works.&#8221;</p><footer>King Alfred's Boethius, chapter 17 &#183; tr. W. J. Sedgefield, 1900 &#183; Alfred's own addition to Boethius &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/King_Alfred's_Version_of_the_Consolations_of_Boethius/The_Consolations_of_Boethius" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;There were so few of them that I cannot remember a single one south of the Thames when I came to the throne.&#8221;</p><footer>Preface to the Pastoral Care &#183; tr. Henry Sweet, 1871 &#183; on the decline of learning in England &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Page:King_Alfred%27s_West-Saxon_Version_of_Gregory%27s_Pastoral_Care_(2).djvu/12" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>1 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;In this we can still see their tracks, but we cannot follow them, and therefore we have lost both the wealth and the wisdom, because we would not incline our hearts after their example.&#8221;</p><footer>Preface to the Pastoral Care &#183; tr. Henry Sweet, 1871 &#183; the words he imagines the old places of learning saying of their forefathers &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Page:King_Alfred%27s_West-Saxon_Version_of_Gregory%27s_Pastoral_Care_(2).djvu/14" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
